      "year": 2019,
      "abstract": "This book is a series of brief, casual meditations \u2013 excursions or ambles, Mooney calls them \u2013 on Thoreau as a \u201cpoet-philosopher\u201d whose major achievement was a lifelong record of awe at the natural world. Mooney\u2019s Thoreau is a mystic of the ordinary: for him observation was a spiritual act. It took him beyond knowledge to experiences of \u201cpresence,\u201d of valuing the world as it is. What Mooney values in Thoreau is precisely this attitude of valuing. He thus attempts a Thoreauvian reading of Thoreau, \u201ca venture in responsiveness and abandonment\u201d that would convey Mooney\u2019s passionate experience of Thoreau\u2019s writing (\uf644\uf646). He seeks to \u201cdeliver its presence and mood, the way it touches and envelops me, the way it effects changes in mood and attunement directly through intimate contact\u201d (\uf644\uf645). Contained within this impressionistic approach is a criticism of contemporary literary study, and specifically \u201cthat sort of critical thinking that only deflates, demotes, or translates into something else\u201d instead of \u201cestablishing why we should bother to value the thing we ardently contextualize or scrutinize\u201d (\uf644\uf647, original emphasis). There is some truth in Mooney\u2019s polemic. The humanities have felt great pressure to prove their seriousness to the scientific\u2013rationalist university and larger culture. If, by some accounts, the question of value is the unique province of the humanities, their very justification for existing, then this can appear sentimental or decadent \u2013 unserious. The choice is stark: if the humanities are not science they must be religion, and they have opted for the former. Of course, to our unending chagrin, the humanities are neither. The demand for \u201cknowledge production\u201d in the humanities is a defense against the ambiguity of the domain of human value \u2013 \u201chow to live, what to do,\u201d to invoke Wallace Stevens. While Mooney is refreshingly candid about his desire for value, he upholds the false choice between science and religion by advocating a worshipful approach to literature. Against rationalistic analysis that murders to dissect, Mooney proposes rapt description that would dignify the sheer presence or givenness of the text. While the folksy, personal tone of his musings is that of a self-proclaimed outsider, Mooney \u2013 a philosophy professor \u2013 might be surprised to learn that the gist of his argument is not as untimely as he thinks. The major trend in literary studies now is away from interpretation toward what Eve Sedgwick, by now famously, called \u201creparative reading.\u201d\uf644 While they couldn\u2019t be further from Mooney\u2019s humanism, the new approaches \u2013 affect theory, reenchantment, New Materialism, sociological reading, among others \u2013 nevertheless share with him a sense that there is something destructive about hermeneutics: that intensely analyzing texts is an act of aggression, a failure to respect the bare fact of their existence. Like Mooney, the new modes of criticism are descriptive in spirit, hands-off. Mooney writes, \u201cin academia, we corral texts, push them through chutes to be tranquilized by theory and packaged as jargon\u201d (\uf646\uf645).",
